    "title": "Proposed Constitutional Amendment to Ban Property Taxes Headed for Full Vote in Tennessee State Senate - Tennessee Star",
    "text": "An effort that would amend the Tennessee Constitution in order to prohibit the General Assembly from authorizing, levying, or collecting property taxes in the future is headed toward a full vote in the Tennessee Senate after being approved on Tuesday by a key committee.\nThe summary provided for Senate Joint Resolution (SJR) 1 by State Senator Ferrell Haile (R-Gallatin) explains it would amend the Tennessee Constitution in order “to prohibit the General Assembly from levying, authorizing, or otherwise permitting a state tax on property.”\nHaile’s resolution, which he submitted ahead of the legislative session last November, would achieve the ban by amending Article 2, Section 28 of the Tennessee Constitution, which authorizes the General Assembly to establish taxes, including property taxes, to specifically state, “the Legislature shall not levy, authorize, or otherwise permit any state tax upon such property.”\nThe Senate Finance, Ways and Means Committee approved SJR 1 with 9 votes in favor, one vote against, and one vote not present. State Senator Jeff Yarbro (D-Nashville) was the sole lawmaker to vote against the resolution.\nA proposed amendment to the Tennessee Constitution must receive the approval of Tennessee voters at the ballot box and the two-thirds approval of both chambers of the General Assembly during separate legislative sessions for it to be ratified.\nA version of the resolution was passed by the Tennessee State House last year, with 81 state lawmakers voting in favor and just one voting against. This means the proposed change to the constitution could go before voters as early as the midterm elections in November 2026.\nTennessee does not currently have a property tax, but prohibiting future lawmakers from using their constitutional power to levy such a tax in the future was nonetheless among the objectives identified by Americans for Prosperity – Tennessee in early 2024, when the conservative policy and activist group said the amendment is an opportunity to “ensure government is not given the ability to expand its power & spending.”\nOther resolutions would amend the constitution to expand the rights of crime victims and remove the right to bail for specific offenses.\n– – –\nTom Pappert is the lead reporter for The Tennessee Star , and also reports for The Pennsylvania Daily Star and The Arizona Sun Times . Follow Tom on X/Twitter . Email tips to pappert.tom@proton.me.",
